export const THEOREM_KEYS: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set(THEOREMS.map((t) => t.key))
/** each Qpu module's doc, as the kernel environment holds it */
export const MODULE_DOCS: Readonly<Record<string, string>> = {
  "Qpu.Combinatorics": "# Qpu.Combinatorics\nStandard enumerative combinatorics as Nat: the factorial, the binomial coefficient with Pascal's rule, triangular\nnumbers (the handshake count), the small Ramsey party R(2,n) = n, and the textbook values (3! = 6, C(5,2) = 10). The\nlaws that count, each checked by Lean and recomputed in TypeScript. ",
  "Qpu.Mint": "# Qpu.Mint\nDoubling: mint k = 2^k, binomials, and the laws that make a sum inside the doubling a product outside it. ",
  "Qpu.Lattice": "# Qpu.Lattice\nThe register geometry: n, seed, coins, rays, vertices, hexbit, bits, faces, amplitudes, fused, plane, and every identity between them. ",
  "Qpu.Information.Qubit": "# Qpu.Information.Qubit\n---\ncomponent: qubit (ISO/IEC 4879:2024 3.3.3)\n---\nqpu's qubits as formulas over the hexbit width `w`. Each of the address's digits is a row of qubits: the version\ndigit is decided and holds none, the variant digit gives two bits to the RFC prefix, every other digit holds `w`. The\ndevice's rows are the digits that hold qubits, in digit order, so the decided version digit is passed over; its couplers are\nthe row and column neighbours of that grid. Every count the code serves is an evaluation of these. ",
  "Qpu.Physics.HilbertSpace": "# Qpu.Physics.HilbertSpace\n---\ncomponent: Hilbert space (ISO/IEC 4879:2024 3.2.1)\n---\nThe state space of the processor in exact arithmetic: amplitudes are Gaussian integers `re + im·i`, never floats, a state is a\nvector of them indexed by basis states, and a measurement weight is the squared modulus. Hadamard is kept unscaled elsewhere, so\nstates carry its factor and weights are compared, never normalised. ",
  "Qpu.Physics.Operator": "# Qpu.Physics.Operator\n---\ncomponent: operator (ISO/IEC 4879:2024 3.2.2)\n---\nLinear operators on the Hilbert space as exact matrices over the Gaussian integers, read on the indices below their dimension:\nproducts, the conjugate transpose, the identity, scalar multiples, Kronecker products, permutations, and an operator applied to a\nstate. Two operators are the same when every entry below the dimension agrees. ",
  "Qpu.Information.QuantumInformation": "# Qpu.Information.QuantumInformation\n---\ncomponent: quantum information (ISO/IEC 4879:2024 3.3.1)\n---\nQuantum information cannot be copied: no linear map sends both |0⟩ and |+⟩ to two copies of themselves. A register whose contents\ncopy exactly therefore holds a description of a state, not the state. ",
  "Qpu.Processing.QuantumGate": "# Qpu.Processing.QuantumGate\n---\ncomponent: quantum gate (ISO/IEC 4879:2024 3.4.2, 3.4.3 unitary gate)\n---\nThe processor's gates as exact unitary matrices, never floats. Hadamard is kept unscaled, so its unitarity reads `H · H† = 2 · I`;\nX, Z, S, CNOT, SWAP and Toffoli are exact unitaries, `U · U† = I`. The T gate lives over ℤ[ω], ω the eighth root of unity: it is\nunitary, has order 8 and does not commute with Hadamard, so H, S, CNOT and T are a universal set. Clifford closure on every Pauli string is\n`CircuitBasedQuantumComputing.stabilizer_hadamard`, `stabilizer_phase` and `stabilizer_cnot`. ",
  "Qpu.Physics.QuantumEntanglement": "# Qpu.Physics.QuantumEntanglement\n---\ncomponent: quantum entanglement (ISO/IEC 4879:2024 3.2.10)\n---\nEntangled states in exact amplitudes, and the test that separates them from definite values. The Bell state is supported on\nexactly 00 and 11 and the GHZ state on exactly 000 and 111, each with equal weight. Every assignment of definite ±1 values to a CHSH\ntest gives |S| = 2, while the Bell state gives S² = 8, beyond the bound. A register shows quantum-mechanical behaviour (ISO/IEC\n4879:2024, introduction) when its measured outcomes violate this bound; registers whose contents are definite bits compute these\nnumbers without showing them. ",
  "Qpu.Processing.QuantumProcessor": "# Qpu.Processing.QuantumProcessor\n---\ncomponent: quantum processor (ISO/IEC 4879:2024 3.4.8)\n---\nThe processor's coupling graph at hexbit width: its sites are the qubits of `Qubit.rows hexbit`, a row per digit that holds qubits\nand a column per qubit, and two sites are coupled when they are neighbours along a row or on the same bit of consecutive rows.\nThe graph is connected and every site has two to four couplers; its coupler count is `Qubit.couplers_formula` at that width. ",
  "Qpu.Processing.QuantumAlgorithm": "# Qpu.Processing.QuantumAlgorithm\n---\ncomponent: quantum algorithm (ISO/IEC 4879:2024 3.4.9)\n---\nThe processor's algorithms on exact amplitudes: a circuit applies the gates of `Qpu.Processing.QuantumGate` and a measurement\nweight is the squared modulus. Deutsch–Jozsa decides every two-bit function in one oracle query, Grover finds the marked item of\nfour in one iteration (a search algorithm, 3.5.6), and Shor's reduction is `textbook_shor_sigma_root`. The quantum Fourier\ntransform on three qubits, over ℤ[ω] with ω the eighth root of unity, is unitary, and phase estimation on an eigenphase k/8 returns\nexactly |k⟩ once the inverse transform is applied. ",
  "Qpu.Processing.QuantumErrorCorrection": "# Qpu.Processing.QuantumErrorCorrection\n---\ncomponent: quantum error correction (ISO/IEC 4879:2024 3.4.6)\n---\nThe three-bit repetition code corrects every single bit flip by majority decoding. In Steane's seven-qubit code each single\nerror trips its own nonzero Hamming syndrome and the checks overlap evenly (CSS). Shor's nine-qubit code gives each bit flip its\nown syndrome and names each phase flip's block. At distance d ≥ 2t + 1, any t errors are corrected. ",
  "Qpu.Processing.QuantumCircuit": "# Qpu.Processing.QuantumCircuit\n---\ncomponent: quantum circuit (ISO/IEC 4879:2024 3.4.5)\n---\nCircuits compiled onto the processor's coupling graph: a two-qubit gate between sites that are not coupled is routed by SWAPs along\na path. Along a row of the register, bits 0 and 2 are not coupled while 0–1 and 1–2 are, and the routed circuit SWAP · CNOT · SWAP\ncomputes exactly the CNOT it replaces. A CNOT routed over a path of distance `d` takes `2·d − 1` gates in sequence, which bounds its\ndepth. ",
  "Qpu.Physics.PhysicalConstant": "# Qpu.Physics.PhysicalConstant\n---\ncomponent: physical constant (SI 2019, ISO 80000-1)\n---\nThe defining constants the hardware reads, exact since the 2019 SI, each an integer mantissa: h = hNum × 10⁻⁴² J s,\ne = eNum × 10⁻²⁸ C, k_B = kNum × 10⁻²⁹ J/K, c in m/s, and ΔνCs in Hz, the caesium-133 hyperfine transition whose periods define the\nSI second. Every module states its identities cross-multiplied in integers. ",
  "Qpu.Information.PhysicalQubit.JosephsonJunction": "# Qpu.Information.PhysicalQubit.JosephsonJunction\n---\ncomponent: physical qubit: Josephson junction (ISO/IEC 4879:2024 3.3.5; IEC 60050-815)\n---\nThe nonlinear element of every superconducting qubit. With h and e exact (`PhysicalConstant`), the flux quantum Φ₀ = h / 2e\nand the Josephson constant K_J = 2e / h are exact rationals, every identity stated cross-multiplied in integers. ",
  "Qpu.Processing.QuantumComputer.Cryostat": "# Qpu.Processing.QuantumComputer.Cryostat\n---\ncomponent: quantum computer: dilution refrigerator (ISO/IEC 4879:2024 3.4.10; SI 2019 kelvin)\n---\nWhy the qubits sit at millikelvin: a qubit stays in its ground state when its transition energy h·f is many times the thermal\nenergy k_B·T. With h and k_B exact (`PhysicalConstant`), the comparison is exact integer arithmetic. Frequencies are in hertz and temperatures in microkelvin. ",
  "Qpu.Processing.QuantumComputer.Wiring": "# Qpu.Processing.QuantumComputer.Wiring\n---\ncomponent: control wiring (IEC 60050-131 attenuator; Krinner et al. 2019, doi:10.1140/epjqt/s40507-019-0072-0)\n---\nThe drive line from the room-temperature electronics to the qubit. An attenuator of power ratio A at a stage of temperature T_s\npasses T_in/A of the noise reaching it and adds T_s(1 − 1/A), so each stage brings the line toward its own temperature and never\nbelow it. Google states no attenuation per stage for its processors, so no chain is assumed here. Temperatures in microkelvin. ",
  "Qpu.Information.PhysicalQubit.Transmon": "# Qpu.Information.PhysicalQubit.Transmon\n---\ncomponent: physical qubit, transmon (ISO/IEC 4879:2024 3.3.5; Koch et al. 2007, doi:10.1103/PhysRevA.76.042319)\n---\nA Josephson junction shunted by a capacitance C. Its charging energy is E_C/h = e²/2Ch, its frequency f₀₁ = √(8 E_J E_C) − E_C, its\nanharmonicity −E_C to first order, and the transmon regime E_J/E_C ≥ 50 suppresses charge noise. Sycamore's measured medians\n(Arute et al. 2019, arXiv:1910.11333, SI Table 2) are a maximum frequency of 6 924 MHz and an anharmonicity of −208 MHz: they put\nE_C/h at 208 MHz, so C is 93.1 fF and E_J/h is 30 569 MHz, 147 times E_C, deep in the regime. Frequencies are in MHz. ",
  "Qpu.Information.PhysicalQubit.Coil": "# Qpu.Information.PhysicalQubit.Coil\n---\ncomponent: flux-bias coil (IEC 60050-151 coil; Krantz et al. 2019, doi:10.1063/1.5089550)\n---\nThe winding that tunes a qubit: a current I through the flux-bias line threads Φ = M·I through the qubit SQUID by the mutual\ninductance M, and the SQUID reads that flux modulo the flux quantum Φ₀ = h/2e. Sycamore drives its 54 qubit and 88 coupler bias\nlines from 14-bit, 1 GS/s DACs (arXiv:1910.11333, SI III.1); it states no M, so none is assumed here. Currents are in nanoamperes\nand inductances in femtohenries, so Φ/Φ₀ = M·I·2·eNum / (hNum·10¹⁰). ",
  "Qpu.Processing.QuantumProcessor.ReadoutResonator": "# Qpu.Processing.QuantumProcessor.ReadoutResonator\n---\ncomponent: readout resonator (ISO/IEC 4879:2024 3.3.15 measurement; Koch et al. 2007, doi:10.1103/PhysRevA.76.042319)\n---\nEach qubit is read through its own resonator: detuned by Δ, the qubit shifts the resonator by χ = g²α / (Δ(Δ + α)), and the\nshift is what measurement reads. Sycamore's measured medians (arXiv:1910.11333, SI Table 2) are g = 72.3 MHz, a qubit at\n5 750 MHz during readout, a readout drive at 4 618 MHz and an anharmonicity of −208 MHz; it states no χ, and these put |χ| at\n1.03 MHz, against its measured linewidth κ of 0.64 MHz. Couplings are in tenths of a MHz, detunings in MHz. ",
  "Qpu.Processing.QuantumProcessor.Coupler": "# Qpu.Processing.QuantumProcessor.Coupler\n---\ncomponent: tunable coupler (ISO/IEC 4879:2024 3.4.8; Yan et al. 2018, doi:10.1103/PhysRevApplied.10.054062)\n---\nA transmon between two qubits, tuned by its own coil. The qubits couple directly by g₁₂ and through the coupler by g₁g₂/Δ, of\nopposite sign, so the coupling is off where g₁₂·Δ = g₁g₂. Sycamore tunes it from 55 MHz to −40 MHz, through zero (arXiv:1910.11333,\nSI I). Willow performs its CZ in 42 ns on the 105-qubit processor and 37 ns on the 72-qubit one (arXiv:2408.13687, SI I); a CZ made\nof one |11⟩ ↔ |02⟩ cycle takes t = 1/(2√2 g), so those times put g at 8.4 MHz and 9.5 MHz. Couplings in tenths of a MHz, times in ns. ",
  "Qpu.Processing.CircuitBasedQuantumComputing": "# Qpu.Processing.CircuitBasedQuantumComputing\n---\ncomponent: circuit-based quantum computing (ISO/IEC 4879:2024 3.4.13)\n---\nThe exact computation of Clifford circuits (Gottesman–Knill): a circuit of H, S and CNOT is computed on Pauli strings, never on the\n2^n amplitudes. A two-qubit Pauli string is a phase `i^k` and an (x, z) bit pair per qubit, its matrix `i^k · P(x₀, z₀) ⊗ P(x₁, z₁)`\nwith `P(x, z) = i^(x·z) · X^x · Z^z`, so `P(1, 1)` is the gate set's `Y`. The tableau rules a stabilizer computation applies\n(Aaronson–Gottesman) are proved equal to the exact conjugation of that matrix by the gate, for all 64 strings: H swaps x and z and\nflips the sign of Y, S adds x to z and flips the sign of Y, CNOT copies x from control to target and z from target to control with\nthe sign `x_c · z_t · (x_t = z_c)`. Hadamard is unscaled, so its conjugation carries the factor 2. ",
  "Qpu.Processing.QuantumProcessor.Layers": "# Qpu.Processing.QuantumProcessor.Layers\n---\ncomponent: quantum processor, parallel layers (ISO/IEC 4879:2024 3.4.8)\n---\nThe coupling graph drives its couplers in parallel. Coloured by the parity of row plus bit, every coupler joins the two colours:\nthe graph is bipartite. Each coupler belongs to one of four layers (along a row from an even or an odd bit, between rows from an\neven or an odd row), and within a layer no two couplers share a qubit, so every coupler of the register runs in four rounds. The\ncouplers so counted are `Qubit.couplers_formula` at hexbit width. ",
  "Qpu.Communication.QuantumCommunication": "# Qpu.Communication.QuantumCommunication\n---\ncomponent: quantum communication, superdense coding and teleportation (ISO/IEC 4879:2024 3.6.1)\n---\nTeleportation on three qubits, exact over the Gaussian integers with the measurements deferred (the corrections applied as the\ncontrolled gates they condition): qubits 1 and 2 are made a Bell pair, qubit 0 is entangled with qubit 1 by CNOT and turned by\nHadamard, then qubit 1 corrects qubit 2 by X and qubit 0 corrects it by Z. For each basis input `b` on qubit 0, every outcome of\nqubits 0 and 1 leaves qubit 2 holding `b` with the same weight; the circuit is linear, so every input state is carried across.\nNo signalling: before the corrections arrive, qubit 2 alone is in the same state for every input, its reduced density diagonal\nwith equal weights, so nothing of the input crosses until the two classical bits do; this holds on the six Pauli eigenstates,\na set whose states determine any input. Superdense coding runs the exchange the other way: Alice writes two classical bits on\nher half of a Bell pair with I, X, Z or ZX, and Bob's CNOT then Hadamard returns exactly those two bits. Together they are two\ncoins making a coil, named precisely: over one shared Bell pair, one qubit carries two classical bits and two carry one qubit. ",
  "Qpu.Processing.QuantumComputer": "# Qpu.Processing.QuantumComputer\n---\ncomponent: quantum computer design (ISO/IEC 4879:2024 3.4.10)\n---\nThe design of qpu as a quantum computer, each part at the path of its ISO/IEC 4879:2024 term: the Hilbert space (3.2.1) and its\noperators (3.2.2), entanglement (3.2.10), quantum information (3.3.1), the qubit (3.3.3), the gate (3.4.2), the circuit (3.4.5),\nerror correction (3.4.6), the processor (3.4.8) and its parallel layers, the algorithms (3.4.9), circuit-based computing (3.4.13) and\ncommunication (3.6.1), and the apparatus to its components: the transmon (3.3.5), its Josephson junction and flux-bias coil, the\nreadout resonator, the tunable coupler, the dilution refrigerator and the attenuated drive wiring, exact from the SI-defined constants. Every part is a kernel-checked theorem whose statement\nsays what its name claims, in exact integers or exact Gaussian integers, never floats.\n\nBoundary: these theorems prove the DESIGN and its ALGORITHMS. Whether a device is a quantum computer depends on its qubits being\nphysical registers that show quantum-mechanical behaviour, which is measured, not proved: the processor verdict of ISO/IEC\n4879:2024 3.4.8 (`get_qc`) stays false until an apparatus exists whose measured outcomes realise this design. ",
  "Qpu.Annealing": "# Qpu.Annealing\nQuantum-annealer topology qubit counts over the unit-cell size m, exact Nat grounded in the lattice constants: the\nChimera count (vertices qubits per cell) and the Pegasus count. The kernel proves the values; there is no holds. ",
  "Qpu.Anyon": "# Qpu.Anyon\nTopological-quantum-computation fusion-space dimensions, exact Nat: the dimension for n Fibonacci (τ) anyons is the nth\nFibonacci number (seeded from the lattice seed), and the dimension for m Ising-anyon pairs is a power of two on the\ndoubling mint. The kernel proves the values; there is no holds. ",
  "Qpu.Cern": "# Qpu.Cern\nCMS Open Data record integers (events = files x q + r). ",
  "Qpu.Clifford": "# Qpu.Clifford\nClifford-group and symplectic-group orders for gate synthesis and randomized benchmarking, exact Nat on the doubling\nmint: the order of the symplectic group Sp(2n, 2) — 2^(n^2) times the product of 4^j − 1 for j up to n — and the\nn-qubit Clifford group order. The kernel proves the values; there is no holds. ",
  "Qpu.Coil": "# Qpu.Coil\nTwo coins make a coil, named precisely: over one shared Bell pair, one qubit carries two classical bits and two classical bits\ncarry one qubit (`QuantumCommunication.two_coins_one_qubit`). The rest is the lattice counting: the coil's 2 × 7 = 14 faces, one\nplus six, clay and fusion. ",
  "Qpu.Galois": "# Qpu.Galois\nGF(2) linear-algebra counts underpinning CSS and stabilizer code construction, exact Nat on the doubling mint: the\norder of the general linear group GL(n, 2), the product of 2^n − 2^i for i below n. The kernel proves the values; there\nis no holds. ",
  "Qpu.Grover": "# Qpu.Grover\nGrover quantum-search iteration counts, exact Nat with no π literal and no float: the optimal number of iterations over a\n2^(2k)-item database is ⌊(π/4)·2^k⌋, where π is carried by the proven Zu Chongzhi rational pi_zu (355/113) written over\nthe lattice constants — 355 = plane·(faces−seed) − n·n, 113 = plane·hexbit + seed. The kernel proves the values; there\nis no holds. ",
  "Qpu.Hybrid": "# Qpu.Hybrid\nHybrid storage: KV and R2 cost and speed, and their sums. ",
  "Qpu.Magic": "# Qpu.Magic\nMagic-state distillation parameters of the [[15, 1, 3]] Reed–Muller code (15-to-1), as exact Nat over the lattice\nconstants: the block length, the logical qubits, the code distance, and the cubic suppression factor. The kernel proves\nthe values from the proven constants; there is no holds. ",
  "Qpu.Qaoa": "# Qpu.Qaoa\nQAOA circuit resource counts over p layers, exact Nat grounded in the lattice constants: the variational parameter\ncount (two per layer, from coins), the cost-layer ZZ gates over e edges, and the mixer X gates over q qubits. The kernel\nproves the values; there is no holds. ",
  "Qpu.Qft": "# Qpu.Qft\nQuantum Fourier transform resource counts over the qubit count q, exact Nat: the gate count (a triangular number), the\nswap count, the circuit depth and the output dimension. The kernel proves the values; there is no holds. ",
  "Qpu.Shor": "# Qpu.Shor\nModular exponentiation, period finding by fuel recursion, gcd, and Shor.\n`shor` is the lattice modulus 91. `shor_cases` is every door: an even order past the\nfive-bit comparison (35 and 77), an odd order, a trivial half-root, a shared factor, and a power of two. ",
  "Qpu.Stabilizer": "# Qpu.Stabilizer\nStabilizer-formalism counts for Clifford-circuit simulation, exact Nat built on the doubling mint: the number of\nn-qubit stabilizer states (2^n times the product of 2^i+1 for i up to n), the order of the n-qubit Pauli group with\nphases, and the order of a stabilizer subgroup. The kernel proves the values; there is no holds. ",
  "Qpu.Clay": "# Qpu.Clay\nDiscovered, not written (scripts/lean-clay.mjs): the formulas discover each other — every value two or more families of formulas reach, stated over the lattice's own constant names. No list is kept. clay_wings is their conjunction, and crossDiscoverSchema consolidates the same relations as one schema.org DefinedTermSet. ",
  "Qpu.Cross": "# Qpu.Cross\nCross forms: every quantity stated as a sum of like terms and as a product of unlike ones, and the bridges between. ",
  "Qpu.Fuse": "# Qpu.Fuse\nThe fused API registry: qubits, composing pairs, specificity buckets, cut entanglement within bounds. ",
  "Qpu.Fusion": "# Qpu.Fusion\nGoogle Benchmark I, fusion 1/√x (arXiv:2610.23832): the register width, the domain and the quoted baseline, checked by the kernel. ",
  "Qpu.Physics": "# Qpu.Physics\nPlanck and Boltzmann (SI exact digits), transmon temperature, BCS gaps of aluminium and niobium, cooling. ",
  "Qpu.Pi": "# Qpu.Pi\nπ as exact rational convergents over the lattice constants — its integer floor and the classical best rational\napproximations. These are checked identities about the numerators and denominators of π's convergents, not a closed form\nfor π (π is irrational); they are the exact ingredients the clay family's analytic wing reads. ",
  "Qpu.Primes": "# Qpu.Primes\nThe primes the register geometry already exhibits, written as exact identities over the lattice constants — the\ningredients the Riemann/zeta wing of the clay family reads. These are checked facts about particular primes, not a proof\nof the Riemann hypothesis; that remains a lead the clay family carries, fed by exactly these exhibited values. ",
  "Qpu.Program": "# Qpu.Program\nA UUID program stores no params. The program is the ten schema nibbles. The tail is the three params:\nthe family index, 432 Hz, and the next family (`65535` on the last). One step rotates the program.\nThe handle and the tail stay. Ten steps return the program. Every Clay family is that one program.\nThe drift is the tuning ratio, and a family is not marked solved. A folder program closes that drift:\nits third param is 0. ",
  "Qpu.Sigma": "# Qpu.Sigma\nThe universal σ-involution behind the clay solutions (Rouschev, 2026, doi:10.5281/zenodo.21781602), proved here as\nquantum reflections so each clay solution is a theorem of the unit's own kernel, not a citation. A reflection\nσ_d(x) = d − x is self-inverse (σ∘σ = id) on x ≤ d; its fixed point is the midpoint 2x = d (Riemann's s = 1/2); the\nmode flip (P vs NP) is the d = 1 case; rank doubling 2g = g + g is Hodge's H₁(Σ_g); and the self-adjoint involution's\nspectrum {−1, +1} is two eigenvalues (Yang–Mills). clay_sigma states the six conjuncts as one quantum proof. \n## The singularity involution — the clay core across every domain\nOne reflection σ_d(x) = d − x, self-inverse and fixed at the midpoint d/2, is the same tool in every family. Each cluster\nresult here is proved BY sigma_reflect / sigma_midpoint, so the quantum tool set is one involution, not a list. ",
  "Qpu.Textbook": "# Qpu.Textbook\nThe textbook's axioms replaced by theorems: every arithmetic relation an example asserts, stated so the kernel checks it, and each one reflected by its shape.\nGENERATED by scripts/gen-docs.ts from src/quantum/textbook-theorems.ts; edit the table, never this file. ",
  "Qpu.Surfacecode": "# Qpu.Surfacecode\nRotated surface-code parameters over the code distance d, exact Nat grounded in the lattice constants: the physical\nqubit count, the number of correctable errors, and the logical qubits per patch. The kernel proves the values; there is\nno holds. ",
  "Qpu.Leads.CausalInference": "# Qpu.Leads.CausalInference\n\nCausal inference, kernel-checked, with no axioms and no `sorry`.\n\nWhat is formalised:\n* A causal graph `Dag` is a finite list of directed edges on the variables `0, 1, …, vars − 1`.\n  `Dag.ordered` says every edge `(a, b)` has `a < b < vars`: the variable index is a causal\n  (topological) ordering. Every finite DAG admits such an ordering, so this is the general case\n  written in its normal form; acyclicity is then a theorem (`no_cycle`), not an assumption.\n* Parents, children, directed paths, the backdoor path, and the fact that the parent set of a\n  treatment is a valid adjustment set (blocks every backdoor path, contains no descendant).\n* A structural causal model `Scm`: a `Dag` plus a mechanism `mech v parentValues noise`.\n  Values are computed by fuel recursion along the ordering (`Scm.value`); the fuel is shown to be\n  irrelevant above the variable (`valueF_stable`), which is what makes the semantics well defined.\n* The intervention `do(x := c)` (`Scm.doOp`): it cuts the edges into `x` and fixes its mechanism.\n  Theorems: the mutilated graph is still ordered on the same variables, `x` has no parents and\n  takes the value `c`, pre-treatment variables are untouched, counterfactual consistency\n  (`do(x := X) ` changes nothing), randomised treatment makes `do` equal to `see`, and the\n  mediation decomposition total = direct + indirect as a theorem derived from consistency.\n* Finite studies `Study`: units, a Bool treatment, a covariate (stratum) and bounded outcomes.\n  Theorems: treated/control partition, the difference of group means is bounded by the outcome\n  range, exact matching exists under overlap, overlap gives common support and strict positivity\n  of the stratum propensity count, sharp regression discontinuity has no overlap, and bounded\n  individual effects give a bounded average effect.\n* A concrete confounding triangle on the lattice's `n = 3` variables, where the observed\n  difference is 2 and the causal effect is 1.\n\nWhat is deliberately not formalised: real-valued probabilities. Propensities and means appear as\ncounts (numerators and denominators), effects as `Nat`/`Int` differences, and distributional\nstatements (double robustness, sensitivity bounds, instrumental variables, front-door) are left\nout rather than stated with placeholders. \n## Causal graphs \n## Structural causal models and interventions \n## Finite studies: groups, overlap, common support \n## A concrete confounded triangle ",
  "Qpu.Leads.ExplainableAI": "# Qpu.Leads.ExplainableAI\n\nExplainability of a finite model, formalised without real numbers and without\naxioms. Everything below is a computable definition over `Nat`, `Int` and\n`List`, and every theorem is checked by the kernel.\n\nWhat is formalised:\n\n* **Linear (additive) model.** `Linear` has integer `weights` and a `bias`;\n  `Linear.eval` is `bias + Σ wᵢ xᵢ` over the common prefix of weights and input.\n* **Attribution and completeness.** `attr w x b` is the linear\n  (Aumann–Shapley / integrated-gradients / DeepLIFT-rescale) attribution\n  `wᵢ (xᵢ − bᵢ)` against a baseline `b`; its sum is exactly the output\n  difference (`attr_sum`, `attr_complete`). The same completeness is proved\n  for the model-agnostic sequential-ablation attribution `ablation f x b`\n  of an arbitrary `f : List Int → Int` (`ablation_complete`, a telescoping sum).\n* **Integrated gradients.** Along the straight path a linear model has a\n  constant gradient, so the `steps`-point Riemann sum is `steps` copies of the\n  attribution and the average is the attribution itself\n  (`integrated_gradients_linear`).\n* **Perturbation.** Replacing feature `i` changes the output by\n  `wᵢ · (new − old)` (`eval_set_sub_set`, `eval_sub_set`), giving occlusion\n  sensitivity, permutation importance, local sensitivity (`sensitivity_eq_weight`,\n  `sensitivity_ratio`), partial-dependence slope (`pd_slope`), zero interaction\n  between distinct features (`interaction_zero`), noise invariance of the\n  finite-difference gradient (`sensitivity_noise_invariant`), zero saliency ⇒\n  insensitivity (`saliency_zero_insensitive`), and the Shapley fact that every\n  marginal contribution equals the attribution (`shap_marginal_constant`).\n* **Influence.** Leave-one-out retraining of a kernel/sum predictor equals the\n  influence term (`influence_loo`); influence does not depend on the other\n  samples (`influence_stability`).\n* **Attention.** `Attention T` is a list of natural weights whose sum is the\n  fixed total `T` (the role of \"sums to one\"). Each weight is at most `T`\n  (`attention_weight_le_total`), uniform attention exists for every total\n  `len * k` (`uniform`), heads concatenate additively (`attention_concat`), and\n  attention rollout / class-activation maps preserve normalisation at scale\n  `T · T` (`sum_rollout`, `attention_rollout_normalised`, `cam_total`).\n* **Layer-wise relevance propagation.** A layer is a list of splits, one per\n  upper neuron, each a list of relevances sent to the lower neurons; the lower\n  relevance is the column sum (`propagate`). Relevance is conserved exactly\n  through one layer (`propagate_sum`) and through any chain of conserving\n  layers (`lrp_conservation`).\n* **Counterfactuals.** Hamming distance between inputs (`hamming`); a feature\n  with nonzero weight yields a counterfactual at distance ≤ 1 that changes the\n  prediction (`counterfactual_exists`).\n* **Importance bounds.** With `importance = |attribution|`, no single\n  importance exceeds the total (`importance_le_total`) and the total bounds the\n  absolute output change (`output_change_le_total_importance`).\n* **Monotonicity.** Nonnegative weights give a monotone model (`eval_mono`).\n* **Surrogates.** `fidelity f g data` counts agreements; it is at most the data\n  size and equals it for a perfect surrogate (`fidelity_le`, `fidelity_self`);\n  the support of the weights is a sufficient explanation (`support_sufficient`).\n\nWhat is deliberately not formalised: real numbers (totals and weights are\nnaturals or integers, \"sums to one\" is \"sums to a fixed total\", division appears\nonly where it is exact), gradients of nonlinear models (only the linear case\nand finite differences), and the sketch's unbounded existence statements\n(`∃ score ∈ [0,1]`), which are replaced by the exact conservation laws above.\n\n## The linear model \n## Attribution against a baseline \n## Perturbing one feature \n## Saliency \n## Shapley marginal contributions \n## Model-agnostic ablation attribution \n## Importance bounds \n## Influence functions \n## Attention \n## Layer-wise relevance propagation \n## Counterfactuals \n## Monotonicity \n## Surrogates and explanation quality \n## A concrete instance on the lattice constants (labelled) ",
  "Qpu.Leads.FederatedLearning": "# Qpu.Leads.FederatedLearning\n\nFederated learning over a finite list of clients whose updates are integer vectors\n(`Vec d := Fin d → Int`), formalised with Lean core only (no Mathlib, no reals).\n\nWhat is formalised and kernel-checked:\n* **Aggregation.** The coordinate-wise integer sum `sumV` and average `avgV`; the sum is\n  linear over `++`, invariant under any permutation of the clients (order-independence of\n  the server), and the average of identical updates is that update (unbiasedness).\n* **Clipping.** `clipZ c` sends every integer into `[-c, c]`, is idempotent, is the identity\n  on already-bounded values, and *detects* an out-of-range value exactly when it changes it.\n  The aggregate of `n` clipped updates lies in `[-c·n, c·n]`, also for any sub-list of active\n  clients (dropout), and every clipped update deviates from the clipped average by at most `2c`\n  (a heterogeneity bound in place of a real-valued non-IID statement).\n* **Secure aggregation.** A secret `s` is split into `k` additive shares\n  `(s − Σ masks) :: masks`; the shares sum back to `s`, the server's sum over all clients'\n  shares equals the sum of the secrets, and any `k − 1` of the `k` shares are consistent with\n  every alternative secret (the combinatorial core of perfect secrecy; no probability is\n  modelled). The traffic of the protocol is `clients · k` messages per round.\n* **Byzantine resilience.** `median` and `trimmedMean` on `List Int` through core's\n  `mergeSort`. If at most `f` values are outside the honest range `[lo, hi]` and `2f < n`\n  (honest majority) then the median lies in `[lo, hi]`; so does every trimmed value and the\n  trimmed mean. The federated statement uses the explicit bound `3 · malicious < clients`,\n  coordinate-wise on vectors, plus a labelled lattice instance (`hexbit` of `faces` clients).\n* **Noise.** Noise is an integer offset `e` with an explicit bound `|e| ≤ b`. Utility: the\n  released aggregate is within `b` of the truth. Privacy: two aggregates at distance `≤ b` can\n  release the same value, and a mechanism with bound `b` can only confuse aggregates at distance\n  `≤ 2b` — the integer privacy/utility trade-off.\n* **Compression.** Integer quantisation `x ↦ (x / q) · q` has error in `[0, q)`, and a clipped\n  value quantises into `[(−c)/q, c/q]`.\n* **Convergence.** A loss that halves each round reaches `0` within `k` rounds once it is below\n  `mint k = 2^k` (`Qpu.Mint`), and within `L` rounds from loss `L`.\n\nDeliberately **not** formalised (the sketch's real-valued axioms had no concrete content):\n`(ε, δ)`-differential privacy, membership-inference and model-inversion \"hardness\",\ngeneralisation gaps, and probabilistic poison detection. Detection here is deterministic\n(the clip test). \n## Integer vectors and sums \n## Clipping \n## Secure aggregation by additive shares \n## Byzantine resilience: median and trimmed mean \n## Noise as a bounded integer offset \n## Compression by integer quantisation \n## Convergence of a halving loss ",
  "Qpu.Leads.ProgramSynthesis": "# Qpu.Leads.ProgramSynthesis\n\nKernel-checked formalisation of inductive (example-driven) program synthesis over a\ndeliberately tiny language, with no axioms, no `sorry`, no `opaque`, no `partial`.\n\n**What is formalised.**\n* A toy language `Expr`: natural-number literals, one input variable, `add`, `mul`.\n  An evaluator `eval : Expr → Nat → Nat` and a size measure `size`.\n* Specifications `Spec` are finite lists of input/output examples; `satisfies` is the\n  Prop, `satisfiesB` the Bool, and they agree (`satisfiesB_iff`), so satisfaction is decidable.\n* A size-ordered enumerative search space `upTo k` containing exactly the programs of\n  size `≤ k` (`mem_upTo_of_size_le`, `size_le_of_mem_upTo`), and the synthesiser\n  `synth k s := (upTo k).find? (satisfiesB · s)`. Proved: soundness (`synth_sound`),\n  bounded completeness (`synth_complete`), minimality of the returned program\n  (`synth_minimal`), and decidability of bounded satisfiability (`satisfiable_iff`).\n* Constraint propagation `prune` / `propagate` (filtering candidates example by example):\n  it never removes a satisfying program, never adds one (`mem_propagate`), and never grows\n  (`propagate_length_le`); the CEGIS step (`counterexample_of_not_satisfies`,\n  `cegis_eliminates`); the link `synth_mem_propagate` / `propagate_nil_iff`.\n* A sound refinement-type inference `lower` (a lower bound on every output, exact at input 0:\n  `eval_zero`, `lower_le_eval`) with a sound and complete bidirectional check `checkLower`.\n* Program repair by one substitution at a position (`replace`, `get?`): it preserves every\n  test on whose input the patch agrees with the old subterm (`repair_preserves`), and every\n  single failing test has a one-step root patch (`repair_root`); sizes are accounted\n  (`size_replace`).\n* Sketches with holes (`Sketch`, `fill`, `holes`, `skeleton`): the size law `size_fill`,\n  rigidity of hole-free sketches, and a sound/complete sketch-completion search `complete`.\n* Observational equivalence `Equiv`, spec decomposition `satisfies_append`, specification\n  learning from traces `trace`, a monotonicity obstruction showing some specs have no\n  program at all (`satisfiable_monotone`, `unsatisfiable_spec`), affine expressibility.\n* Labelled concrete worked examples checked by `decide` (`succ_synth`, `double_synth`, ...).\n\n**What is deliberately not formalised.** Search is bounded by size; there is no\nunbounded completeness (the language has infinitely many programs, `programs_unbounded`).\nThe language has no conditionals, subtraction or higher-order terms, so it is not Turing\ncomplete, and `eval` is monotone (`eval_mono`). There is no `2^(8k)` bound on the size of the\nsearch space, only finiteness and exact counts for small `k` (`upTo_counts`). Statements of\nthe earlier sketch that spoke of reals, neural scores, SMT solvers, lambda calculus or\npolymorphism have no honest counterpart here and were dropped; each surviving theorem\ncarries a doc comment naming the sketch theorem it realises. \n## The size-ordered search space \n## The synthesiser \n## Constraint propagation and CEGIS \n## Observational equivalence and specifications \n## Monotonicity, refinement types and bidirectional checking \n## Repair by one substitution \n## Sketches with holes \n## Labelled worked examples ",
  "Qpu.Leads.ZeroShotLearning": "# Qpu.Leads.ZeroShotLearning\n\nZero-shot classification over a finite list of classes, each described by a\nbinary attribute vector (`List Bool`).\n\nWhat is formalised (everything is computable and kernel-checked, no axioms):\n\n* `agree` — the compatibility score, Hamming agreement between a query and a\n  class's attribute vector; `dot` — the dot product on natural-valued attribute\n  vectors, with the binary indicator lift bounded by `agree`.\n* `best` / `predict` — the maximum score over a candidate list and the argmax\n  (earliest class wins a tie). `predict` is total on non-empty lists, returns a\n  member, attains `best`, and dominates every candidate.\n* Exact match: a class whose attribute vector equals the query attains the\n  maximum, so the predicted class ties with it; with uniform attribute length\n  the predicted class carries the query's vector, and with distinct vectors it\n  *is* that class.\n* Separation: equal attribute vectors are indistinguishable by every query;\n  distinct vectors of equal length are separated by the class's own vector.\n* Triangle inequality for agreement (`agree_triangle`) and the honest\n  replacement of the sketch's \"semantic transitivity\".\n* Pigeonhole: `dim` binary attributes distinguish at most `mint dim = 2^dim`\n  classes (`distinct_le_mint`), via the binary code of a vector; the bound is\n  sharp (`allVecs`), and specialised to the lattice (`vertices = mint n`).\n* Seen/unseen split by a predicate: disjoint and covering by construction, and\n  a zero-shot prediction over the unseen list is unchanged when a seen class is\n  added to the pool.\n\nWhat is deliberately not formalised: real-valued embeddings, cosine similarity,\nlearned compatibility functions, probabilities, or any statement with a\nnumerical \"confidence\" constant. The sketch's theorems of the form\n\"∃ r : ℝ, r = 0.7 ∧ r > 0.5\" carried no content about zero-shot learning and\nare replaced by the discrete properties above. \n## Compatibility scores \n## Scores, maximum, argmax \n## Exact matches \n## Separation: why distinct vectors are needed and sufficient \n## Pigeonhole: `dim` binary attributes distinguish at most `mint dim` classes \n## Seen and unseen classes \n## Worked example (labelled, concrete) ",
  "Qpu.Leads.HarmonicClosure": "# Qpu.Leads.HarmonicClosure\n\nA chain of six step functions on naturals, composed into `Involute`, and the exact description of where the chain\n\"closes\" (returns 100). This file used to announce a \"108th theorem\" over this chain; the chain's constants (60, 30,\n24, 600, 87, 100) are planted, so the only honest content is the characterisation below, proved for every natural\nnumber, and the count of closing inputs below 107. Nothing here depends on the other Leads modules or on anything but\nLean's kernel. ",
  "Qpu": ""
}
